/** Standalone batch Y -- new 4-case standard (Sample + boundary + tricky + stress).
 * All solvers below were independently written and verified byte-for-byte against each problem's
 * real official Sample before being trusted to generate boundary/tricky/stress expected output,
 * EXCEPT uva-441 whose official Sample was a placeholder (see below).
 *
 * uva-437-the-tower-of-babylon: classic 3x-orientation-expansion + longest-strictly-increasing-base
 *   chain DP (sorted by base area, dp[i] = tallest tower ending with orientation i). Verified against
 *   the real sample's own case 1 (a single non-cube block type can still stack on ITSELF via two
 *   different orientations, since supply is unlimited) and confirmed via a standalone cube case (a
 *   perfect cube's 3 orientations are all identical, so nothing can ever stack, max height = the side
 *   length only).
 * uva-439-knight-moves: plain BFS over the 8x8 board graph with the 8 standard knight-move deltas.
 * uva-441-lotto: THE SCRAPED OFFICIAL SAMPLE IS NOT REAL DATA -- its expected output is literally the
 *   placeholder string "Please generate output by the sample code.", not actual judge output. This is
 *   a trivial fixed C(k,6) combination enumerator in ascending lexicographic order; verified instead
 *   directly against the problem statement's own worked example (k=8, S={1,2,3,5,8,13,21,34} -> 28
 *   games, with the first 4 games matching the statement's own listing exactly). sampleLimit: 0.
 * uva-442-matrix-chain-multiplication: recursive-descent parser over the "(Expr Expr)" | "Matrix"
 *   grammar, propagating an "error" flag up through every enclosing parenthesis the instant any inner
 *   dimension mismatch is found (verified with a case where the mismatch is 2 levels deep, alongside an
 *   independently-dimensioned valid deep-nesting case to confirm errors don't leak between expressions).
 * uva-455-periodic-strings: try every divisor k of the string length in increasing order, checking
 *   whether every k-length block equals the first; the smallest such k is the period (falls back to the
 *   full string length when no smaller period exists, i.e. an all-distinct-character string).
 * uva-458-the-decoder: shift each character by -7 within the 95-character printable ASCII range
 *   [32,126] inclusive (space through '~'), wrapping around within that range -- confirmed against the
 *   real sample (a stray apostrophe decoding to a literal space is only possible if the wrap range
 *   INCLUDES space, not just 33-126) and additionally round-trip-verified across the ENTIRE printable
 *   range via a matching +7 encoder used only to build this file's own boundary/tricky/stress input.
 * uva-482-permutation-arrays: x'[p_i] = x[i] (place each input value at the position given by its
 *   permutation entry); output values are copied as the ORIGINAL input token strings, never
 *   reparsed/reformatted through a float, so formats like trailing ".0" or a fixed decimal-digit count
 *   are preserved byte-for-byte exactly as given.
 */
